// Continental Europe-Maghreb: the Continental European synchronous area with the
// Maghreb systems that run in step with it (spec section 28.3; research note
// docs/research/2026-10-18-world-power-grids-top-8.md, section "B -
// Continental Europe-Maghreb"): the continent from Portugal to Turkiye with
// western Denmark (DK1), connected Ukraine and Moldova, the Baltic states,
// Sicily, Malta and Crete, and Morocco, northern interconnected Algeria and
// Tunisia through the Spain-Morocco AC link. The notes exclude the Nordic
// area, eastern Denmark (DK2), Great Britain, Ireland, Sardinia, Corsica,
// separately operated islands and isolated southern Algeria; ENTSO-E
// membership alone is not the boundary.
//
// Grid-operation rulings:
// - DK1 is in and DK2 is out: Natural Earth admin-0 DNK less the 1:10m
//   admin-1 regions Hovedstaden (with Bornholm) and Sjaelland (Zealand,
//   Lolland-Falster), so Nordjylland, Midtjylland and Syddanmark (Jutland and
//   Funen) remain.
// - Out as separately operated systems: Sardinia (the eight 1:10m admin-1
//   provinces), Corsica (Haute-Corse and Corse-du-Sud), the Balearics
//   (Baleares) and the Canaries (Las Palmas and Santa Cruz de Tenerife).
//   Cyprus, Great Britain, Ireland, the Nordic countries, Russia and Belarus
//   are not selected. The smaller non-interconnected Aegean islands (Lesbos,
//   Rhodes, Chios and the like) fall under the extractor's 2-cell land floor.
// - Crimea and Sevastopol are out (spec section 28.3, user decision
//   2026-10-25). Natural Earth's 1:50m admin-0 UKR already leaves Crimea to
//   RUS (checked: neither Simferopol nor Sevastopol lies in UKR's polygons),
//   and the canonical extent (crates/infrastructure/authoring/
//   ukraine_ips_operating_extent.ron) is pasted verbatim as an exclusion
//   (ruling 11). Mainland Ukraine, Donetsk and Luhansk included, is in.
// - Isolated southern Algeria is out, with a schematic boundary at 30 N: the
//   In Salah-Adrar-Timimoun pole (PIAT) is a separate southern system that a
//   planned double 400 kV Hassi R'mel-Timimoun line would join to the
//   national interconnected network (RIN), and a Ghardaia-Tamanrasset line is
//   only under consideration (Enerdata, "Sonelgaz launches a 700 km
//   transmission project to link Algeria's power grids", 2023-05-04). The
//   exclusion is 1:10m admin-1 Adrar, Tamanghasset, Tindouf, Bechar, Ouargla,
//   Illizi and Ghardaia south of 30 N, plus 1:50m admin-0 DZA south of 30 N
//   for the Morocco-border seam the admin-1 cut misses.
// - Areas the notes don't address (spec section 28.3):
//   - Transnistria is in. Its Moldavskaya GRES supplied about 70% of
//     Moldova's electricity before Moldova's grid synchronised with
//     Continental Europe on 16 March 2022 (Moldova Ministry of Energy, "How
//     Has the Energy Sector Transformed with the Boost of Renewable Energy?",
//     2025-09-19, section "Synchronization with the Continental Grid Was a
//     Game-Changer", paragraphs 2-4). It lies inside admin-0 MDA.
//   - Kosovo is in (ADM0 KOS). KOSTT began operating as a regulatory zone
//     of the Continental European system, in the Kosovo-Albania block, when
//     its ENTSO-E connection agreement took effect on 14 December 2020
//     (KoSSev, "KOSTT starts operation as a regulatory zone within the
//     Kosovo-Albania bloc", 2020-12-14).
//   - Western Sahara is in from the Moroccan border south to Dakhla, west of
//     the berm, which Natural Earth's admin-0 MAR outline already contains.
//     Sourced ONEE grid operation:
//     - Laayoune: the 470 km Tiznit-Laayoune line (Le Matin, "Mise en service
//       d'une nouvelle ligne electrique Tres Haute Tension reliant Tiznit a
//       Laayoune", 2015-08-04). Laayoune joined the 225 kV national grid in
//       1998, which let Smara Province be connected in 2000 (FNH, "Entretien.
//       Les regions du Sud du Maroc beneficient d'un gisement en energies
//       renouvelables exceptionnel", interview with ONEE's Director General,
//       2021-09-01, first answer, first paragraph).
//     - Boujdour and the Aftissat wind farm at 400 kV since February 2018
//       (EcoActu, "Raccordement de Dakhla au reseau electrique: le DG de
//       l'ONEE s'enquiert de l'etat d'avancement des travaux", 2021-02-26).
//     - Dakhla: a 400 kV double-circuit line now reaches the city (Le360,
//       "Info360. Lancement imminent d'une nouvelle liaison electrique entre
//       Boujdour et Marrakech", 2025-11-03, paragraph beginning "A signaler
//       que le reseau").
//     The rest of the interior west of the berm is in by authoring judgment:
//     administrative extent west of the berm; only the coastal backbone
//     (Tiznit-Laayoune-Boujdour-Dakhla) and Smara Province are sourced.
//     Out: the part east of the berm (Natural Earth's separate ADM0 SAH
//     feature, not selected) and everything south of 23.55 N, a schematic
//     exclusion just south of Dakhla (23.70 N) listed after the generated
//     rings, where no source shows the grid.
//
// Geography: tools/region_authoring/selections/continental_europe_maghreb.args
// holds the extractor arguments: Natural Earth v5.1.2 1:50m admin-0 for 41
// countries (Kosovo by ADM0 KOS), dissolved (ruling 8), with the 1:10m
// admin-1 exclusions above. Regenerate with
//   tr '\n' '\0' < tools/region_authoring/selections/continental_europe_maghreb.args \
//       | xargs -0 python3 -I tools/region_authoring/natural_earth.py
// which reports 12 land and 22 exclude rings. The land rings are the
// mainland (Iberia to Ukraine and Thrace), Anatolia, the Maghreb, Sicily,
// Crete, Euboea, Funen, Saaremaa, and Zealand, Mallorca, Corsica and Sardinia
// (each under its exclusion). The selection's exclusions dissolve into 26
// rings, 23 of which meet the bounds; 22 come out (ruling 9). The one that
// collapses is a 4-point Haute-Corse islet (9.46-9.48 E, 42.62-42.65 N) that
// lies in no included land ring, so no land is re-admitted. The southern
// Algeria run (continental_europe_maghreb_algeria_south.args, extent mode)
// reports 1 exclude ring from 1 dissolved ring meeting its bounds. Its 1:10m
// admin-1 edge leaves a seam against the 1:50m Morocco-Algeria border, so a
// second southern-Algeria ring follows it: the 1:50m admin-0 DZA ring of the
// economic-zone authoring file (python3 -I tools/region_authoring/
// natural_earth.py --admin0 target/natural-earth/ne_50m_admin_0_countries.geojson
// --include ISO:DZA --bounds=-16.65,44.95,22.87,59.87 --grid 154,123
// --tolerance-deg 0.02 --dissolve, which reports 1 land and 0 exclude rings)
// clipped to 22.87-30.0 N, 40 points. The exclude list holds 38 rings: the
// 22 and 1 generated, that derived ring, the canonical Crimea extent, the ten
// IPS/UPS seam cells and three schematic exclusions.
// Tolerance: 0.02 degrees in all three runs, the same as assets/regions/ips_ups.ron
// and the canonical Crimea extent, so the shared IPS/UPS borders (Ukraine,
// Moldova, the Baltic states, Poland and Turkiye against Russia, Belarus and
// Georgia) are the same 1:50m admin-0 lines simplified alike.
// IPS/UPS seam (ruling 10): the same borders are not enough, because a cell
// is playable when its centre is, and an IPS/UPS land cell (1.0 x 0.5
// degrees) reaches up to half a cell past the border. Ten IPS/UPS land cells
// held 14 Europe-Maghreb land centres, and one IPS/UPS centre fell in a
// Europe-Maghreb land cell, so those ten IPS/UPS cells are excluded here, after
// the generated rings; with them no centre is playable in both regions.
// Nudge rings after the generated land put coastal cities and starter plants
// whose cell centres fall offshore on land, and give Malta, Crete and Sicily
// a land cell at Valletta, Heraklion and Palermo. Schematic exclusions open
// the Strait of Messina, cut Western Sahara south of Dakhla and take out the
// one Libyan cell centre at the Ghadames tripoint that the simplified outline
// admits. The grid's
// rows are placed so a row of centres lies in the Strait of Gibraltar, so
// the starter link crosses one water cell.
// Points are (lon, lat) in degrees.
//
// Grid: 16.65 W to 44.95 E and 22.87 to 59.87 N on 154 x 123 cells (0.4 x
// 0.3008 degrees). At the middle latitude, 41.37 N, a cell is 33.38 km
// east-west by 33.45 km north-south (aspect 1.002, cell_km 33.414).
//
// Money is in EUR at local price levels from the Plan 7 research note
// (docs/research/2026-10-25-plan-7-regional-economics.md), converted to game
// values by its pacing method. Each entry's comment cites its rows and shows
// the arithmetic; the ratios use full precision (ruling 25). The catalog is
// economic zone 0, the regional default, from the note's "Europe-Maghreb
// economic-zone proposal": German technology-level capital (P7-DE-001 to
// P7-DE-007, midpoints) with pan-European ERAA O&M and fuel. Spain (zone 1)
// and Morocco (zone 2) override wind and solar construction (Spain's wind
// from a 2025 market average by user decision, the rest from whole projects),
// and northern Algeria (zone 3) and Tunisia (zone 4) override solar and CCGT
// construction from projects (the note's "Algeria and Tunisia zone proposal
// (follow-up 2026-10-26)"); see the economic_zones block. Countries on zone 0
// by authoring choice (the note's "Regional default by authoring choice"):
// Portugal, France, Belgium, the Netherlands, Luxembourg, Germany,
// Switzerland, Austria, Italy (with Sicily), Malta, Slovenia, Croatia, Bosnia
// and Herzegovina, Serbia, Montenegro, North Macedonia, Albania, Greece (with
// Crete), Bulgaria, Romania, Hungary, Slovakia, Czechia, Poland, Lithuania,
// Latvia, Estonia, Denmark (DK1), Ukraine, Moldova (with Transnistria),
// Turkiye, Kosovo, Andorra, Monaco, Liechtenstein, San Marino and Vatican
// City.
(
    region_schema: 4,
    id: "continental_europe_maghreb",
    system_zone: 2,
    // Civil clocks (spec section 28.4). The EU zones observe summer time from
    // April to October (most days of each month). Central European Time is the
    // dispatcher's clock; Morocco, Algeria and Tunisia keep UTC+1 all year
    // (Morocco's Ramadan shift is not modelled), and Turkiye keeps UTC+3.
    time_zones: [
        (id: 1, name_key: "zone-wet", standard_offset_minutes: 0, dst_months: [false, false, false, true, true, true, true, true, true, true, false, false]),
        (id: 2, name_key: "zone-cet", standard_offset_minutes: 60, dst_months: [false, false, false, true, true, true, true, true, true, true, false, false]),
        (id: 3, name_key: "zone-cet-fixed", standard_offset_minutes: 60, dst_months: [false, false, false, false, false, false, false, false, false, false, false, false]),
        (id: 4, name_key: "zone-eet", standard_offset_minutes: 120, dst_months: [false, false, false, true, true, true, true, true, true, true, false, false]),
        (id: 5, name_key: "zone-trt", standard_offset_minutes: 180, dst_months: [false, false, false, false, false, false, false, false, false, false, false, false]),
    ],
    // Northern meteorological seasons.
    calendar: (seasons: [Winter, Winter, Spring, Spring, Spring, Summer, Summer, Summer, Autumn, Autumn, Autumn, Winter]),
    currency: EUR,
    atlas: (colour: "#226EC7"),
    name_key: "region-continental-europe-maghreb",
    bounds: (west: -16.65, east: 44.95, south: 22.87, north: 59.87),
    grid: (width: 154, height: 123),
    // Generated rings (spec section 28.11.1), each sidecar written by its selection in
    // tools/region_authoring/selections, whose header records the source data and command:
    // natural_earth.ron (continental_europe_maghreb.args); algeria_south.ron
    // (continental_europe_maghreb_algeria_south.args), isolated southern Algeria with a
    // schematic boundary at 30 N, 1:10m admin-1 Adrar, Tamanghasset, Tindouf, Bechar, Ouargla,
    // Illizi and Ghardaia dissolved and clipped to 9 W-12 E, 22.87-30 N; and the canonical
    // Crimea and Sevastopol extent, ukraine_ips_operating_extent.ron
    // (ukraine_ips_operating_extent.args, ruling 11).
    geography: ["natural_earth.ron", "algeria_south.ron", "ukraine_ips_operating_extent.ron"],
    land: [
        // Nudge ring: Lisbon, whose cell centre falls in the Tagus estuary.
        [
            (-9.280, 38.633), (-9.120, 38.633), (-9.120, 38.755), (-9.280, 38.755),
        ],
        // Nudge ring: Barcelona, offshore centre.
        [
            (2.153, 41.340), (2.380, 41.340), (2.380, 41.413), (2.153, 41.413),
        ],
        // Nudge ring: Marseille, offshore centre.
        [
            (5.340, 43.145), (5.580, 43.145), (5.580, 43.326), (5.340, 43.326),
        ],
        // Nudge ring: Aarhus (DK1), centre in Aarhus Bay.
        [
            (10.170, 56.080), (10.380, 56.080), (10.380, 56.190), (10.170, 56.190),
        ],
        // Nudge ring: Naples, centre in the Bay of Naples.
        [
            (14.219, 40.738), (14.380, 40.738), (14.380, 40.866), (14.219, 40.866),
        ],
        // Nudge ring: Sicily: Palermo (belt and braces; its centre is already land in the generated outline).
        [
            (13.330, 38.031), (13.580, 38.031), (13.580, 38.150), (13.330, 38.150),
        ],
        // Nudge ring: Malta: Valletta (the Maltese islands fall under the 2-cell land floor).
        [
            (14.320, 35.870), (14.540, 35.870), (14.540, 35.985), (14.320, 35.985),
        ],
        // Nudge ring: Crete: Heraklion, centre in the Sea of Crete.
        [
            (25.110, 35.310), (25.180, 35.310), (25.180, 35.384), (25.110, 35.384),
        ],
        // Nudge ring: Rabat, offshore centre.
        [
            (-6.880, 33.991), (-6.811, 33.991), (-6.811, 34.180), (-6.880, 34.180),
        ],
        // Nudge ring: Algiers, centre in the Bay of Algiers.
        [
            (3.057, 36.703), (3.180, 36.703), (3.180, 36.888), (3.057, 36.888),
        ],
        // Nudge ring: Tunis, centre in the Gulf of Tunis.
        [
            (10.152, 36.776), (10.380, 36.776), (10.380, 36.888), (10.152, 36.888),
        ],
        // Nudge ring: the Palos combined-cycle plant (starter fleet), centre in the Gulf of Cadiz.
        [
            (-6.923, 37.129), (-6.820, 37.129), (-6.820, 37.214), (-6.923, 37.214),
        ],
        // Nudge ring: the Campo de Gibraltar combined-cycle plant (starter fleet), centre in the Mediterranean off San Roque.
        [
            (-5.423, 36.153), (-5.220, 36.153), (-5.220, 36.286), (-5.423, 36.286),
        ],
        // Nudge ring: the Tahaddart combined-cycle plant (starter fleet), centre in the Atlantic off Tahaddart.
        [
            (-6.080, 35.559), (-5.957, 35.559), (-5.957, 35.685), (-6.080, 35.685),
        ],
    ],
    exclude: [
        // 1:50m admin-0 DZA south of 30 N, the Morocco-border seam the admin-1 cut misses: the
        // 1:10m admin-1 outline in algeria_south.ron runs up to about 0.25 degree south of the 1:50m
        // Morocco-Algeria border, which left four playable Algerian cell centres at 29.6-29.9 N
        // (5.2-6.5 W). This is the zone-3 DZA ring (crates/infrastructure/authoring/
        // continental_europe_maghreb_economic_zones.ron, same extractor run) clipped to
        // 22.87-30.0 N, so the cut and the zone come from one source scale.
        [
            (-5.394, 30.000), (-5.449, 29.957), (-6.004, 29.831), (-6.480, 29.820), (-6.521, 29.660),
            (-6.635, 29.569), (-7.142, 29.620), (-7.486, 29.392), (-7.685, 29.350), (-8.265, 28.981),
            (-8.678, 28.689), (-8.683, 27.286), (-4.823, 24.996), (-1.558, 22.870), (10.869, 22.870),
            (11.968, 23.518), (11.508, 24.314), (10.686, 24.551), (10.396, 24.486), (10.256, 24.591),
            (10.120, 24.790), (10.028, 25.051), (10.001, 25.332), (9.448, 26.067), (9.422, 26.147),
            (9.438, 26.246), (9.491, 26.334), (9.859, 26.552), (9.883, 26.631), (9.894, 26.848),
            (9.837, 26.916), (9.795, 27.045), (9.748, 27.331), (9.916, 27.786), (9.858, 28.043),
            (9.816, 28.560), (9.843, 28.967), (9.805, 29.177), (9.640, 29.636), (9.389, 30.000),
        ],
        // The IPS/UPS seam (ruling 10): each rectangle is one IPS/UPS land cell (its 1.0 x 0.5
        // degree grid, assets/regions/ips_ups.ron) whose area reaches across the shared
        // Natural Earth border and holds a Europe-Maghreb cell centre or contains the cell
        // around an IPS/UPS centre. Excluding them leaves no cell centre playable in both.
        // 27-28 E, 58.5-59 N.
        [
            (27.000, 58.500), (28.000, 58.500), (28.000, 59.000), (27.000, 59.000),
        ],
        // 27-28 E, 58-58.5 N.
        [
            (27.000, 58.000), (28.000, 58.000), (28.000, 58.500), (27.000, 58.500),
        ],
        // 27-28 E, 55.5-56 N.
        [
            (27.000, 55.500), (28.000, 55.500), (28.000, 56.000), (27.000, 56.000),
        ],
        // 23-24 E, 52-52.5 N.
        [
            (23.000, 52.000), (24.000, 52.000), (24.000, 52.500), (23.000, 52.500),
        ],
        // 27-28 E, 51.5-52 N.
        [
            (27.000, 51.500), (28.000, 51.500), (28.000, 52.000), (27.000, 52.000),
        ],
        // 30-31 E, 51.5-52 N.
        [
            (30.000, 51.500), (31.000, 51.500), (31.000, 52.000), (30.000, 52.000),
        ],
        // 35-36 E, 50.5-51 N.
        [
            (35.000, 50.500), (36.000, 50.500), (36.000, 51.000), (35.000, 51.000),
        ],
        // 39-40 E, 49.5-50 N.
        [
            (39.000, 49.500), (40.000, 49.500), (40.000, 50.000), (39.000, 50.000),
        ],
        // 39-40 E, 47.5-48 N.
        [
            (39.000, 47.500), (40.000, 47.500), (40.000, 48.000), (39.000, 48.000),
        ],
        // 43-44 E, 41-41.5 N.
        [
            (43.000, 41.000), (44.000, 41.000), (44.000, 41.500), (43.000, 41.500),
        ],
        // The Strait of Messina: a schematic channel so the 3 km strait shows as one water
        // cell between Calabria and Sicily (spec section 28.8: the crossing is a submarine
        // cable). It takes the cell that holds both Messina and Reggio Calabria.
        [
            (15.530, 38.050), (15.680, 38.050), (15.680, 38.300), (15.530, 38.300),
        ],
        // Western Sahara south of Dakhla: a schematic cut at 23.55 N. ONEE's 400 kV line ends
        // at Dakhla (23.70 N), and no source shows its grid farther south. The cut keeps the
        // grid row that holds Dakhla's latitude (23.47-23.77 N, centres at 23.62 N) and takes
        // the two rows below it (centres at 23.32 and 23.02 N), 11 land cells. Dakhla's own
        // cell (on its peninsula, off the 1:50m coast) is water with or without the cut.
        [
            (-17.000, 22.500), (-12.000, 22.500), (-12.000, 23.550), (-17.000, 23.550),
        ],
        // Libya at the Ghadames tripoint: simplification admits one LBY centre. The cell at
        // 9.35-9.75 E, 30.09-30.39 N has its centre (9.55 E, 30.24 N) in Libya in the
        // unsimplified 1:50m data, but inside the simplified Maghreb outline. A schematic
        // square inside that cell only takes it out.
        [
            (9.450, 30.150), (9.650, 30.150), (9.650, 30.330), (9.450, 30.330),
        ],
    ],
    // Relief: the Alps, the Pyrenees, the Atlas, the Carpathians, the Dinaric
    // and Balkan ranges, the Apennines, the Iberian Meseta and the Anatolian
    // plateau; the North European Plain and the Pontic lowlands are low. Named
    // ranges only; heights are an authoring judgment (no elevation dataset was
    // used).
    height: (
        base: 0.15,
        blobs: [
            // The Alps.
            (lon: 10.0, lat: 46.5, radius_deg: 1.8, value: 0.55),
            // The Pyrenees, the Massif Central and the Betic ranges.
            (lon: 0.5, lat: 42.6, radius_deg: 1.0, value: 0.4),
            (lon: 2.8, lat: 45.2, radius_deg: 1.0, value: 0.2),
            (lon: -3.3, lat: 37.1, radius_deg: 0.8, value: 0.3),
            // The Iberian Meseta.
            (lon: -4.0, lat: 40.0, radius_deg: 3.0, value: 0.15),
            // The High and Middle Atlas, and the Tell Atlas and Aures.
            (lon: -6.0, lat: 31.8, radius_deg: 1.5, value: 0.45),
            (lon: 5.5, lat: 35.5, radius_deg: 2.0, value: 0.2),
            // The Apennines, the Dinaric Alps and the Balkans, and the
            // Carpathians.
            (lon: 13.5, lat: 42.5, radius_deg: 1.5, value: 0.25),
            (lon: 19.0, lat: 43.0, radius_deg: 2.0, value: 0.3),
            (lon: 24.5, lat: 46.5, radius_deg: 2.5, value: 0.3),
            // The Anatolian plateau and eastern Anatolia.
            (lon: 34.0, lat: 39.0, radius_deg: 4.0, value: 0.3),
            (lon: 41.0, lat: 39.5, radius_deg: 2.0, value: 0.45),
            // The North European Plain and the Pontic lowlands.
            (lon: 15.0, lat: 53.0, radius_deg: 5.0, value: -0.1),
            (lon: 32.0, lat: 48.5, radius_deg: 4.0, value: -0.05),
        ],
    ),
    // Sunshine as site quality without latitude (spec section 28.5), from the
    // annual sunshine totals in English Wikipedia's "List of cities in Europe
    // by sunshine duration" and "List of cities by sunshine duration" (Africa
    // and Asia tables), read 2026-10-25. Each annual total is a share of the
    // possible hours at its latitude (FAO-56 equations 24, 25 and 34 on the
    // middle day of each month), turned into irradiance by the
    // Angstrom-Prescott relation (FAO-56 equation 35, a = 0.25, b = 0.50).
    // For example: Brussels 0.426, Paris 0.440, Berlin 0.436, Kyiv 0.473,
    // Vienna 0.484, Bucharest 0.491, Istanbul 0.503, Ankara 0.530, Rome
    // 0.532, Madrid 0.566, Athens 0.567, Algiers 0.575, Valletta 0.599,
    // Seville 0.624 and Ouarzazate 0.640. The base is north-western Europe's
    // level; the blobs are a least-squares fit of those 27 city values at
    // fixed, authored centres (rms error 0.018; placement is an authoring
    // judgment, and no irradiance atlas was read). Maghreb solar shows here:
    // southern Morocco is the region's sunniest land.
    irradiance: (
        base: 0.44,
        blobs: [
            // The Guadalquivir valley and the Iberian interior.
            (lon: -6.0, lat: 37.4, radius_deg: 1.5, value: 0.058),
            (lon: -6.5, lat: 39.5, radius_deg: 3.0, value: 0.16),
            // Southern Morocco (Ouarzazate and Marrakesh).
            (lon: -7.0, lat: 31.0, radius_deg: 2.0, value: 0.199),
            // Malta and Sicily, and the Algerian and Tunisian coast.
            (lon: 14.5, lat: 36.0, radius_deg: 2.0, value: 0.156),
            (lon: 5.0, lat: 36.5, radius_deg: 3.0, value: 0.162),
            // Crete and the Aegean.
            (lon: 25.0, lat: 36.5, radius_deg: 2.5, value: 0.136),
            // Central Anatolia.
            (lon: 32.0, lat: 39.0, radius_deg: 3.0, value: 0.062),
            // Central Italy.
            (lon: 12.5, lat: 42.0, radius_deg: 2.0, value: 0.086),
            // The lower Danube and the Black Sea coast.
            (lon: 28.0, lat: 45.5, radius_deg: 4.0, value: 0.062),
        ],
    ),
    // Water for thermal plants: the Atlantic, North Sea, Mediterranean,
    // Aegean, Marmara and Black Sea coasts, and the Rhine, Rhone, Loire, Seine,
    // Elbe, Danube, Dnieper, Ebro, Tagus, Guadalquivir, Po and Vistula. Blobs
    // sit on the Natural Earth coastline and the named rivers (authoring
